# SPA history fallback for hosting platforms that respect this file.
#
# Netlify is the platform this rule is written for: it honours a 200-status
# rewrite here. Cloudflare Pages already serves the root `index.html` for a path
# that matches no asset when no custom 404 page is deployed, so it needs nothing
# from this file and ignores rules it does not support. Vercel ignores the file
# entirely -- see `vercel.json`, which carries the same fallback plus the
# `public/_headers` security headers.
#
# Why this file has to exist: the app is a BrowserRouter SPA whose routes are
# real URLs -- `/auth/callback`, `/legal/:doc`, `/trips/:id`, `/settings` and the
# rest of the tab routes. None of them exist as files in `dist`, so a static host
# answers a direct navigation to them with its own 404 and the app never boots.
#
# `/auth/callback` is the one that makes this release-blocking rather than
# cosmetic: `authRedirectUrl()` in `src/lib/platform.ts` hands Supabase
# `${window.location.origin}/auth/callback`, so EVERY web OAuth and magic-link
# sign-in lands on that URL as a fresh top-level navigation.
#
# The service worker does not cover for it. `public/sw.js` handles navigations
# network-first and falls back to the cached shell only when `fetch` REJECTS; a
# 404 is a resolved response, so the host's 404 page is what the user sees.

# Existing files still win: Netlify serves a real asset (`/assets/*`, `/sw.js`,
# `/offline.html`, `/manifest.json`) before applying this rule, which is why the
# status is 200 and NOT forced with `!`.
/*    /index.html    200
